/* G coerces a copy of its argument, a list of a and b, to q1 (lists of c
   and d), which fails, but only as the argument of H, which discards it; G
   is passed to the unknown function f of the main type.  The language is
   call-by-name, so the coercion is never checked, and every output is
   c (d e), in q1: sat.  The reduction's coercion problem checks the
   coercion where it is written and refutes, so the answer is unknown,
   never unsat. */
%BEGING
F f g x -> f G x.
G y -> c (H (coerce q1 (Copy y))).
H z -> d e.
Copy x -> case x of
	 e => e
| a x' => a (Copy x')
| b x' => b (Copy x')
| c x' => c (Copy x')
| d x' => d (Copy x').
%ENDG

%BEGINA
q0 a -> q0.
q0 b -> q0.
q0 e -> .
q1 c -> q1.
q1 d -> q1.
q1 e -> .
%ENDA

%BEGINT
F : ((q0 -> q1) -> q0 -> q1) -> (q0 -> q1) -> q0 -> q1.
%ENDT
